.TH RENUMSEQ 1 "2026" "renumseq 3.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
renumseq \- renumber and rename image-sequence frame ranges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B renumseq
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
[\fISEQ\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B renumseq
renumbers the frame range of each
.I SEQ
listed on the command line, and can also rename a sequence's descriptive
name.
.I SEQ
must be specified using
.BR lsseq (1)\|'s
native format:
.PP
.RS
.I descriptiveName\fR.[\fIframeRange\fR].\fIimgExtension
.RE
.PP
Enclosing
.I SEQ
in quotes turns off the shell's special treatment of
.RB \(aq [ \(aq
and
.RB \(aq ] \(aq .
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq
aaa.[001\-005].tif
$ renumseq \-o 10 'aaa.[001\-005].tif'
$ lsseq
aaa.[011\-015].tif
$ renumseq \-\-rename bbb 'aaa.[011\-015].tif'
$ lsseq
bbb.[011\-015].tif
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B renumseq
was written to be safe: if renumbering a sequence would overwrite an
existing file outside the range being renumbered, that sequence is skipped
(with a warning) rather than clobbered; see
.B \-\-force
below to override this. Renumbering is done with a single
.BR rename (2)
call per file (no temporary copies are made), so it is fast, and, by
default, leaves file timestamps untouched \(em see
.B \-\-touch
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Miscellaneous options
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show the help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the program's version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-silent ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress all errors and warnings.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; all subsequent arguments are treated as positional
.I SEQ
arguments, even if they begin with a dash.
.SS Renumbering sequences
.TP
.BI \-\-start " START_FRAME"
Use
.I START_FRAME
as the first frame number of each
.IR SEQ .
Takes precedence over
.BR \-\-offset .
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " FRAME_OFFSET\fR, \fB\-o " FRAME_OFFSET
Offset
.I SEQ
by this number of frames (can be negative). Frame
.I i
becomes
.IR i " + " FRAME_OFFSET .
.TP
.BI \-\-pad " PAD"
Set the padding of the frame numbers to
.I PAD
digits. (Default: leave the padding unchanged.)
.SS Overwrite protection
.TP
.B \-\-skip
If renumbering a file in
.I SEQ
would overwrite an existing file that isn't also being renumbered, skip
renumbering that
.I SEQ
altogether. (Default.) The opposite of
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.B \-\-force
If renumbering a file in
.I SEQ
would overwrite an existing file that isn't also being renumbered,
overwrite it anyway. The opposite of
.BR \-\-skip .
.SS Renaming sequences
.TP
.BI \-\-rename " NEW_SEQNAME"
Rename the descriptive-name part of
.I SEQ
from its existing name to
.IR NEW_SEQNAME .
Exits with an error unless exactly one
.I SEQ
is being renamed and/or renumbered, and never renames to an
already-existing sequence.
.TP
.B \-\-replace\-underscore
If
.I SEQ
uses an underscore
.RB ( _ )
separating the descriptive name from the frame number, replace it with a
dot
.RB ( . )
when renumbering. An offset of zero (which is the the default) only changes the
separator, leaving the frame numbers themselves unchanged.
.SS Additional run-time behavior
.TP
.BR \-\-verbose ", " \-v
List the mapping from old file name to new file name.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Don't renumber
.IR SEQ ;
just display how the files would have been renumbered. Implies
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.BI \-\-touch " \fR[[CC]YYMMDD[\-hh[mm[ss]]]]"
Update the timestamp of the files being renumbered. With no date given,
set it to the current time; otherwise use the date provided. The optional
.I CC
(century) defaults to the current century, and
.IR hh ", " mm ", and " ss
default to zero if omitted. Default (no
.B \-\-touch
at all): leave the timestamp of
.I SEQ
unchanged, the same way
.BR mv (1)
does.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit codes are combined bitwise, so a single run can report more than one
condition at once.
.TP
.B 0
No error.
.TP
.B 1
Attempted to rename a sequence to one that already exists.
.TP
.B 2
Bad command-line argument (the standard argparse error code).
.TP
.B 4
Warning: nothing to do (no offset, rename, or padding change requested).
.TP
.B 8
Warning: an invalid frame range was specified for a sequence.
.TP
.B 16
Warning: an argument was expected to be a sequence but doesn't appear to
be one.
.TP
.B 32
Warning: a specified sequence does not exist.
.TP
.B 64
Warning: renumbering a sequence would have overwritten a frame outside
the range specified; that sequence was skipped (see
.BR \-\-force ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Offset a sequence by 10 frames:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-o 10 'aaa.[001\-005].tif'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Retarget a sequence to start at frame 995, and repad to 4 digits:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-\-start 995 \-\-pad 4 'aaa.[1\-10].jpg'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Do the same while also renaming the sequence:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-\-start 995 \-\-pad 4 \-\-rename bbb 'aaa.[005\-015].tif'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Replace an underscore separator with a dot, without changing frame numbers:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-\-replace\-underscore 'ccc_[5\-15].jpg'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Preview a rename/renumber without touching any files:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-\-dry\-run \-\-offset 50 \-\-rename xxx 'aaa.[001\-010].jpg'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Force overwriting files that would otherwise block a renumber:
.RS
.nf
$ renumseq \-\-force \-o 30 'a.[0100\-0110].jpg'
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORY
.B renumseq
follows Semantic Versioning 2.0.0.
.PP
As of
.BR 2.0.0 ,
all long options were renamed from camelCase to kebab-case (for example,
.B \-\-replaceUnderscore
became
.BR \-\-replace\-underscore ),
a breaking change reflected in the major version bump.
.PP
As of
.BR 3.0.0 ,
the
.B \-s
short form of
.B \-\-silent
was removed, another breaking change, freeing
.B \-s
for reuse as shorthand for
.B \-\-start
in a future release.
